WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.531
Here is a sealed interface for payment methods.

2
00:00:05.531 --> 00:00:08.269
Sealed means it lists every subtype it allows.

3
00:00:08.269 --> 00:00:10.412
Right now that is Card and Transfer.

4
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:14.233
Two pieces of code turn a method into a receipt label.

5
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.820
Both compile, and both give the same answers today.

6
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.537
Now a new payment method ships: Wallet.

7
00:00:20.537 --> 00:00:22.813
You add it to the sealed interface.

8
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.724
Then you recompile. javac reads the permits list, so it knows every Method there can be.

9
00:00:28.724 --> 00:00:32.172
It checks the switch against that list, case by case.

10
00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:36.329
There is no case for Wallet, so the switch is now a compile error.

11
00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:38.573
The chain compiles without a word.

12
00:00:38.573 --> 00:00:44.395
To the compiler it is just a few if statements, and nothing says they must cover every Method.

13
00:00:44.533 --> 00:00:47.356
At run time, a Wallet payment walks through it.

14
00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:48.613
Not a Card.

15
00:00:48.613 --> 00:00:50.085
Not a Transfer.

16
00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:54.156
So it lands in the else, and every wallet receipt says unknown.

17
00:00:55.067 --> 00:01:02.135
Kotlin's when over a sealed interface gets the same check, whether you use it as an expression or as a statement.

18
00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:07.552
Add Wallet, and both compilers stop. kotlinc even names the branch you forgot.

19
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:14.689
Every switch over Method gets this check, so the build stays red until each one handles Wallet.

20
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:16.558
One rule follows.

21
00:01:16.558 --> 00:01:19.832
Don't put a default on a switch over a sealed type.

22
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.641
A default catches Wallet quietly, the same way the else did, and the compiler has nothing left to check.
